<p>Flood damage insurance claims in San Marcos are different from almost every other property claim. The policy is usually separate from your homeowners coverage, the rules come largely from the federal flood program, and the deadlines are strict. Whether water came from the Blanco River, the San Marcos River, a creek like Sink or Purgatory, or sheets of runoff across a neighborhood, the first questions are the same: what kind of water was it, and which policy responds? As a <a href="/">San Marcos public adjuster</a>, we help homeowners and businesses answer those questions and assemble a complete claim.</p>

<h2>Flood History Along the Blanco and San Marcos Rivers</h2>
<p>Hays County sits along the Balcones Escarpment, and NOAA lists 127 flash-flood reports for the county, most often in June, May, and October. Several floods shaped how the community thinks about risk:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Memorial Day flood, May 23&ndash;24, 2015:</strong> the Blanco River at Wimberley rose from about 5 feet to about 41 feet in roughly four hours, and the San Marcos River near San Marcos crested at 43.08 feet. The National Weather Service issued Flash Flood Emergencies for Wimberley and San Marcos (<a href="https://www.fema.gov/disaster/4223" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FEMA DR-4223</a>).</li>
<li><strong>October 30&ndash;31, 2015:</strong> two-day rainfall reached 18.46 inches near Buda and 15.63 inches north of San Marcos (<a href="https://www.fema.gov/disaster/4245" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FEMA DR-4245</a>).</li>
<li><strong>Halloween flood, October 30&ndash;31, 2013:</strong> 12 to 14 inches fell from Wimberley to Driftwood (<a href="https://www.fema.gov/disaster/4159" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FEMA DR-4159</a>).</li>
<li><strong>October 17&ndash;18, 1998:</strong> the NWS estimated more than 30 inches of rain over a small area south of San Marcos in 36 hours (<a href="https://www.fema.gov/disaster/1257" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FEMA DR-1257</a>).</li>
</ul>
<p>Since 2015, the city has used federal HUD CDBG-DR funding for flood mitigation, including the Blanco River Flood Mitigation Project. Our <a href="/flood-damage-insurance-claims-san-marcos/">San Marcos flood claims article</a> covers the history and the claim process in more depth.</p>

<h2>Flood Maps and NFIP Coverage in San Marcos</h2>
<p>The City of San Marcos participates in the National Flood Insurance Program. New FEMA flood maps covering the Blanco River, the San Marcos River, and portions of Sink, Purgatory, Willow Springs, and Cottonwood Creeks took effect on January 17, 2025, according to the city&rsquo;s <a href="https://www.sanmarcostx.gov/402/FEMA-Flood-Map-Revision-Process" target="_blank" rel="noopener">flood map revision page</a>. If your property&rsquo;s zone changed, your lender requirements and premium may have changed too.</p>
<p>Flood zones are about risk, not coverage. A home outside a mapped high-risk zone can still flood, and many flood claims in Texas come from properties that were not required to carry flood insurance.</p>

