<h2>Common Issues With Flood Claims in Hays County</h2>
<h3>Flood versus other water</h3>
<p>Rising water from outside is generally flood. A burst pipe or a roof leak is usually a homeowners claim. When a storm causes both, each insurer may point at the other, so documenting where the water entered is critical. See our <a href="/services/water-damage-claims/">water damage claims</a> page for the non-flood side.</p>
<h3>Strict proof-of-loss timing</h3>
<p>NFIP policies require a signed proof of loss within a set period after the loss. Policyholders who wait for the adjuster&rsquo;s report can run out of time.</p>
<h3>Underestimated drying and tear-out</h3>
<p>Slab-on-grade homes, common in San Marcos subdivisions, absorb water into drywall, baseboards, cabinets, and insulation. A scope that only replaces the lowest few inches may not match the actual waterline.</p>
<h3>Depreciation on contents</h3>
<p>Many flood policies pay contents at actual cash value, which reduces the payment for older items. Our <a href="/replacement-cost-vs-actual-cash-value-texas/">RCV versus ACV guide</a> explains the difference.</p>

<h2>Our Flood Claim Process</h2>
<ol>
<li>Confirm which policies apply and calendar every deadline.</li>
<li>Document waterlines, damaged materials, and building systems before tear-out where possible.</li>
<li>Build a contents inventory and a line-item building estimate.</li>
<li>Prepare and submit the proof of loss with supporting documentation.</li>
<li>Follow up with the flood adjuster and pursue additional payment requests when more damage is found.</li>
</ol>

<h2>When to Call a Public Adjuster About Flooding</h2>
<p>Consider calling a public adjuster in San Marcos if the proof-of-loss deadline is approaching and you do not yet have a complete estimate, if the flood adjuster&rsquo;s scope stops below your waterline, if your contents list is overwhelming, or if your homeowners insurer and flood insurer disagree on the cause. If the same storm also brought wind or hail, our <a href="/services/storm-damage-claims/">storm damage claims</a> page explains how to handle both. For a no-cost first look, request a <a href="/free-claim-review/">free claim review</a>.</p>
